### Stack Overgram Client
The Stack Overgram client is built using React and TypeScript to build a dynamic web app resembling Stack Overflow. Throughout the devleopment process, we have added new pages and components, enhanced functionality of existing pages, and updated the styling of previous pages and components. To do so effectively, we focused on reusing components as much as possible to maintain consistency and reduce redundancy across the application. This approach helps in maintaining a clean and manageable codebase. In addition to the creation of new pages, we also updated the styling of the website to be cleaner, and resemble more modern web applications and social medias.

### Setup of frontend
Generally, most pages are composed of multiple components that may or may not be reusable. In all components, the business logic is separated from the actual html & JSX that make up what the user sees in custom hooks.

### Component Reuse
Some places where we made sure to create reusable components were our question component and our feed & notification pages:

#### Question Component: 
The Question component is used to accomplish multiple functions, including on the Profile page to display different aspects of a user. This ensures that the display and behavior of questions remain consistent throughout the application.

#### Feed and Notification Pages: 
Both the Feed and Notification pages rely on shared components and styling to display lists of follower activities and notifications. This reuse of components helps in maintaining a uniform look and feel across different parts of the application that have similar functionality.

### Setup and Installation
1. Clone the repository and navigate to the client directory

    `git clone https://github.com/neu-cs4530/fall24-team-project-group-112.git`

    `cd client`

2. Install dependencies `npm install`

3. Set up environment variables by creating a .env file in the /client directory. Add the secrets provided by our project group.

4. Run the client 
   
   `npm run start`

5. Access the client: The client will be available at `http://localhost:3000`